/**
\page nodes Nodes

At first you need to decide which type of node you want to add to Yoyo. There are four big
categories:
- Data
- Gui
- Protocol
- Communication

The set of data nodes should be complete as provided by Yoyo, but at least there is the possibility
to add new data nodes to Yoyo if necessary.

\section nodes_gui Creation of Gui nodes
To define a new type of Gui nodes, you need to create a class, which is derived from yoyo::gui_node,
which is in turn derived from yoyo::node_base.

A lot of functionality is already provided by these two classes and doesn't need to be implemented
by derived classes. One example is tree management. Handling addition and removal of children from
a general perspective is already implemented in yoyo::node_base and must not be done by derived
classes.

\subsection nodes_gui_properties Properties
There are already some properties present in yoyo::gui_node. They are accessible through the
respective getters and setters. If you need to set them do not use the constructor to do so, but
override the yoyo::gui_node::init() method (don't forget to call the base class's init method
first).

yoyo::gui_node::init() creates the widget and will be called by the factory directly after creation.

New properties do not fall under this restriction and may be modified at any time.

Use the Q_PROPERTY macro to define your new properties as in

\code{.cpp}
  Q_PROPERTY(yoyo::properties::script_t myScript READ myScript WRITE setMyScript NOTIFY myScriptChanged)
\endcode

\see https://doc.qt.io/qt-5/properties.html for more information on the Qt property system

If you want the property to be persisted or modifiable in the Property Widget, you need to define
READ, WRITE and NOTIFY attributes.

\subsection nodes_gui_childwidgets Adding child widgets
When creating a widget to add it to the gui_node, do not create it providing the widget as parent to
the constructor. Use something like this instead:
\code{.cpp}
void myGuiNode::init()
{
  yoyo::gui_node::init();
  _myButton = new QPushButton;
  addWidget(_myButton);
}
\endcode

Again, as mentioned with the yoyo::gui_node properties, adding or removing widgets must be done
after init has been called.

Use yoyo::gui_node::removeWidget() to remove the widget again if necessary or have the destructor
cleanup automatically for you.

\subsection nodes_gui_container Using a container widget
The widget inside yoyo::gui_node defines already a layout (thus the layout direction property), and
widgets added there are already added to and managed by the layout.

In some cases, it might be suitable to have an other widget used as a container for widgets to add.
This could be a separate frame or a button-group. Again an additional layout is not necessary,
yoyo::gui_node handles this already according to the current value of the layout direction property.

The only thing to do is to actually provide the container widget.

\subsection nodes_gui_childnodes Reacting on tree management
As already mentioned child management is done in yoyo::node_base, and adding child widgets to the
widget is done in yoyo::gui_node. What remains is the dedicated features necessary for your
individual node.

\warning Do not store shared_ptr to children in your implementation. It might screw up the deletion
process. Use weak_ptr if you really must.

There are five private purely virtual methods defined in yoyo::gui_node. You will need to implement
them all, even if only empty.

\subsection nodes_gui_example Example code
\subsubsection nodes_gui_example_header Header
Let's create a gui node with a single button on it. The button shall have a title and when the
button is clicked, a script shall be invoked. This makes two properties, one for the title and one
for the script to be invoked:

\code{.cpp}
  Q_PROPERTY(QString title READ title WRITE setTitle NOTIFY titleChanged)
  Q_PROPERTY(yoyo::properties::script_t onClick READ onClick WRITE setOnClick NOTIFY onClickChanged)
\endcode
For the properties three methods are necessary each: a getter (READ), a setter (WRITE) and a signal
(NOTIFY):
\code{.cpp}
public:
  auto title() const->QString;
  auto setTitle(QString const&t)->void;

  auto onClick() const->properties::script_t;
  auto setOnClick(properties::script_t s)->void;

Q_SIGNALS:
  void titleChanged(QString t);
  void onClickChanged(properties::script_t s);
\endcode
Then we need to implement the purely virtual methods from the base classes:
\code{.cpp}
public:
  auto staticTypeId() const->boost::uuids::uuid final;
  auto supportsValueType(types::value_t valueType) const->bool final;
  auto acceptsChildren() const->bool final;
  auto acceptsChild(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child) const->bool final;
  auto acceptsParent(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&parent) const->bool final;
private:
  auto beforeChildAdd(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child)->bool final;
  auto afterChildAdd(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child)->bool final;
  auto beforeChildRemove(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child)->bool final;
  auto afterChildRemove(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child)->bool final;
  auto afterChildMoved(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child, size_t from_index, size_t to_index)->void final;
\endcode
As you can see, I added the final keyword to the method declarations. It is not necessary, strictly
speaking, to do so, because the whole class is going to be declared final, but this enables the
compiler to tell us if we made a mistake with the method signature.

Since we want to add a widget to the node (the button), we need to override the yoyo::gui_node::init()
method as well, where we can add the button to the widget after it is initialized:
\code{.cpp}
public:
  auto init()->void final;
\endcode

Last but not least, we add in a constructor and a destructor. The destructor cannot be default
implemented, because we have a member with a type, that is only forward declared (QPushButton) and
the destructor if generated in the header won't be able to delete the object.

Complete header:
\code{.cpp}
#pragma once
#include <yoyo/node_gui.h>

class QPushButton;

class my_gui_node final: public yoyo::gui_node
{
  Q_OBJECT

  Q_PROPERTY(QString title READ title WRITE setTitle NOTIFY titleChanged)
  Q_PROPERTY(yoyo::properties::script_t onClick READ onClick WRITE setOnClick NOTIFY onClickChanged)
public:
  explicit my_gui_node(boost::uuids::uuid identifier = boost::uuids::uuid {});
  ~my_gui_node() override;
  auto init()->void final;

  auto staticTypeId() const->boost::uuids::uuid final;
  auto supportsValueType(types::value_t valueType) const->bool final;

  auto title() const->QString;
  auto setTitle(QString const&t)->void;

  auto onClick() const->properties::script_t;
  auto setOnClick(properties::script_t s)->void;

  static auto typeId()->boost::uuids::uuid;

  auto acceptsChildren() const->bool final;
  auto acceptsChild(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child) const->bool final;
  auto acceptsParent(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&parent) const->bool final;

Q_SIGNALS:
  void titleChanged(QString t);
  void onClickChanged(properties::script_t s);

private:
  auto beforeChildAdd(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child)->bool final;
  auto afterChildAdd(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child)->bool final;
  auto beforeChildRemove(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child)->bool final;
  auto afterChildRemove(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child)->bool final;
  auto afterChildMoved(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&child, size_t from_index, size_t to_index)->void final;
private:
  static boost::uuids::uuid const _typeId;
  std::unique_ptr<QPush_button> _button;
  QString _title;
  properties::script_t _script;
};
\endcode

\subsubsection nodes_gui_example_source Implementation
The implementation of the tree management methods is rather unspectacular:
\code{.cpp}
auto my_gui_node::acceptsChildren() const->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::acceptsChild(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&) const->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::acceptsParent(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&) const->bool { return true; }
auto my_gui_node::beforeChildAdd(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&)->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::afterChildAdd(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&)->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::beforeChildRemove(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&)->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::afterChildRemove(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&)->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::afterChildMoved(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&, size_t, size_t)->void {}
\endcode
As well as the implementation of the yoyo::gui_node methods:
\code{.cpp}
boost::uuids::uuid const button::_typeId = boost::uuids::string_generator{} ("9d3dfb69-62f6-4a15-97b6-cf32726d69e2");
auto my_gui_node::typeId()->boost::uuids::uuid { return _typeId; }
auto my_gui_node::staticTypeId() const->boost::uuids::uuid { return _typeId; }

auto my_gui_node::supportsValueType(types::value_t) const->bool { return false; }
\endcode
And implementing the property methods might look like this:

\code{.cpp}
auto my_gui_node::title() const->QString { return _p->_title; }
auto my_gui_node::setTitle(QString const&t)->void
{
  if (_title == t){
    return;
  }
  _title = t;
  _button->setText(_title);
  Q_EMIT titleChanged(_title);
}

auto my_gui_node::onClick() const->properties::script_t { return _p->_script; }
auto my_gui_node::setOnClick(properties::script_t s)->void
{
  if (_script == s) {
    return;
  }
  _script = s;
  Q_EMIT onClickChanged(_script);
}
\endcode
This leaves us with construction and destruction:
\code{.cpp}
my_gui_node::my_gui_node(boost::uuids::uuid identifier)
    : yoyo::gui_node{"my_gui_node", nullptr, identifier}
    , _button{std::make_unique<QPushButton>()}
{
    _button->setObjectName("__pushButton");
    connect(_button.get(), &QPushButton::clicked, this,
      [this]() {
        if (!_script._text.isEmpty()) {
          Q_EMIT scriptExecutionRequested(_script);
        }
    });
}
my_gui_node::~my_gui_node() = default;

void my_gui_node::init()
{
    yoyo::gui_node::init();
    addWidget(_button);
}
\endcode

This could be the source file as a whole:
\code{.cpp}
#include "my_gui_node.hpp"

#include <QPushButton>

my_gui_node::my_gui_node(boost::uuids::uuid identifier)
    : yoyo::gui_node{"my_gui_node", nullptr, identifier}
    , _button{std::make_unique<QPushButton>()}
{
    _button->setObjectName("__pushButton");
    connect(_button.get(), &QPushButton::clicked, this,
      [this]() {
        if (!_script._text.isEmpty()) {
          Q_EMIT scriptExecutionRequested(_script);
        }
    });
}
my_gui_node::~my_gui_node() = default;

void my_gui_node::init()
{
    yoyo::gui_node::init();
    addWidget(_button);
}

auto my_gui_node::title() const->QString { return _p->_title; }
auto my_gui_node::setTitle(QString const&t)->void
{
  if (_title == t){
    return;
  }
  _title = t;
  _button->setText(_title);
  Q_EMIT titleChanged(_title);
}

auto my_gui_node::onClick() const->properties::script_t { return _p->_script; }
auto my_gui_node::setOnClick(properties::script_t s)->void
{
  if (_script == s) {
    return;
  }
  _script = s;
  Q_EMIT onClickChanged(_script);
}

boost::uuids::uuid const button::_typeId = boost::uuids::string_generator{} ("9d3dfb69-62f6-4a15-97b6-cf32726d69e2");
auto my_gui_node::typeId()->boost::uuids::uuid { return _typeId; }
auto my_gui_node::staticTypeId() const->boost::uuids::uuid { return _typeId; }
auto my_gui_node::supportsValueType(types::value_t) const->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::acceptsChildren() const->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::acceptsChild(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&) const->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::acceptsParent(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&) const->bool { return true; }
auto my_gui_node::beforeChildAdd(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&)->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::afterChildAdd(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&)->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::beforeChildRemove(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&)->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::afterChildRemove(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&)->bool { return false; }
auto my_gui_node::afterChildMoved(std::shared_ptr<node_base> const&, size_t, size_t)->void {}
\endcode
\section nodes_protocol Creation of Protocol nodes
\subsection nodes_protocol_properties Properties
\subsection nodes_protocol_children Child management

\section nodes_communication Creation of Communication nodes
\subsection nodes_communication_properties Properties
\subsection nodes_communication_children Child management

\section nodes_data Creation of Data nodes
Creating data nodes should not be necessary from a functionality point of view, since yoyo should
cover the necessary types already. However it is still supported to design own data nodes within
a plugin.

For data nodes there are three possible base classes available:
- yoyo::data_node: the general any purpose data-node
- yoyo::data_value_node: a leaf node specialization for the data node. Might be interesting, because
  tree management methods don't need to be overridden anymore.
- yoyo::data_container_node: an inner node specialization for the data node. Might be interesting
  for inner nodes accepting all kinds of children.

\subsection nodes_data_types Data variants
Each data node has a specific value type, which specifies the type of the data it can hold. Please
consult the enumeration yoyo::types::value_t for a list of supported value types.

\subsection nodes_data_properties Properties
As the previous nodes, your specialization of a data node might need additional configurable
properties to ensure wanted functionality.

There are already some properties present in yoyo::data_node or the provided convenience classes.
They are accessible through the respective getters and setters. Unlike yoyo::gui_node, you can
modify the properties already during construction.

Use the Q_PROPERTY macro to define your new properties as in

\code{.cpp}
  Q_PROPERTY(yoyo::properties::script_t myScript READ myScript WRITE setMyScript NOTIFY myScriptChanged)
\endcode

\see https://doc.qt.io/qt-5/properties.html for more information on the Qt property system

If you want the property to be persisted or modifiable in the Property Widget, you need to define
READ, WRITE and NOTIFY attributes.

Some property types are not meant for the use in data nodes. These are mainly the \ref
properties_supported_types_connectivity. They will work in the sense, that they are persisted and
displayed in the Property Widget. But the connectivity engine will not perform a connection, since
connections between data nodes are not allowed.

\subsection nodes_data_children Child management
As already mentioned child management is done in yoyo::node_base. What remains is the dedicated
features necessary for your individual node.

\warning Do not store shared_ptr to children in your implementation. It might screw up the deletion
process. Use weak_ptr if you really must.

There are five private purely virtual methods defined in yoyo::node_base. You will need to implement
them all, even if only empty. In the convenience classes, you don't even need to do this.

\note When you add Qt connections to children in one of the add-methods, please make sure, the
connections are released again in the corresponding remove-method.

\subsection nodes_data_example Example
Let's see how a leaf node containing a simple boolean might be implemented.

As base class we choose yoyo::data_value_node, which already implements the tree management as we'd
like it and it also provides some properties, which we could make use of.
\subsubsection nodes_data_example_header Header

\code{.cpp}
#pragma once

#include "yoyo/data_value_node.h"

class my_data_node final: public data_value_node
{
  Q_OBJECT

public:
  explicit my_data_node(boost::uuids::uuid identifier = boost::uuids::uuid {});
  ~my_data_node() override = default;

  auto staticTypeId() const->boost::uuids::uuid final;
  auto value() const->types::variant_t final;
  auto setValue(QVariant const&value)->void final;
  static auto typeId()->boost::uuids::uuid;

private:
  static boost::uuids::uuid const _typeId;
  bool _bufferedValue{ false };
};
\endcode
Even though not necessary, it was chosen to add the final keyword to the overridden methods, to
provide compiler checks whether the signature matches the one in the base class.

Since yoyo::data_value_node alread defines properties like title, unit, access-control and an
onChange script, there is nothing left for us to add. What is left is overriding the getter and
setter for the propagated value.

\subsubsection nodes_data_example_source Implementation
This could be a possible implementation:
\code{.cpp}
#include "my_data_node.hpp"

#include <QVariant>

#include <boost/uuid/string_generator.hpp>

boost::uuids::uuid const my_data_node::_typeId = boost::uuids::string_generator{} ("2f39d8d4-1da6-4e9f-81d1-e22c48c1bd8f");
auto my_data_node::typeId()->boost::uuids::uuid { return _typeId; }
auto my_data_node::staticTypeId() const->boost::uuids::uuid { return _typeId; }

my_data_node::my_data_node(boost::uuids::uuid identifier)
    : data_value_node("my_data_node", identifier, types::value_t::BIT)
{
    setUnit({ "", false });
}

auto my_data_node::value() const->types::variant_t { return _bufferedValue; }
auto my_data_node::setValue(QVariant const&value)->void
{
  if (static_cast<QMetaType::Type>(value.type()) != QMetaType::Bool) {
      return;
  }
  if (_bufferedValue == value.toBool()) {
      return;
  }

  _bufferedValue = value.toBool();
  doValueChanged(value);
}
\endcode
*/
